task: schedulers-family
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/schedulers
worktree: ../okay-wt-prof

why: the operator's direction (2026-09-07, after drive-scheduler-jvm
  measured the fork/join gap as the JDK pool's): schedulers the way
  queues are done — a family, each with its own named properties, a
  builder facade to choose by, an adaptive policy where the choice
  depends on what the program turns out to do, AND one scheduler in
  the family that is not slower than kyo's.

what:
  1. WHY kyo is faster, by profile not by guess: JMH stack profile of
     okayDrive / rawPool / kyo (sbt-free launcher), then the
     mechanism named in one sentence with the frames that carry it.
  2. `Schedulers.own` — a scheduler that owns its threads, kyo's
     shape: N workers, a queue each, woken only when idle, external
     submit to the less-loaded of two, steal when empty, `DriveTask`
     the unit. Prototype first, measured on forkJoin10k before
     anything else is built on it.
  3. the facade: `Schedulers.loom` / `.pool(...)` / `.own(...)` /
     `.threads`, each with the properties a caller chooses by —
     blocking-safe (a parked fiber costs a thread?), fibers per
     thread, fairness/preemption, cancellation kind, cost per fork —
     in the scaladoc and in a table in docs/modules.
  4. `Schedulers.adaptive`: the policy that picks by what the
     program does (blocking seen → loom; short CPU fibers → own),
     the way Queues.adaptive picks parts by producers seen. Spec
     first (specs/schedulers.md), laws before mechanism.

predict: (1) the pool's cost is worker park/unpark per task
  (Unsafe.park/unpark, signalWork in the top frames of rawPool and
  okayDrive; kyo's top frames in its Queue lock and IOTask.eval, not
  in park). (2) own workers on forkJoin10k: 2310 -> under 1000 if
  (1) holds; above 1500 disqualifies (1) and says the cost is in
  Drive's walk. (3)/(4) are shape, not speed: no lane moves.
